FEX's offline compiler generates at most N caches per process-all (FEX 3bec2ac, on top of 0027)

process-all --max-caches N generates at most N code caches and leaves the
rest for the next process-all, which picks up where this one stopped (a
cache counts as up to date once it is newer than its ready code map). It
ends with "[process-all] G caches generated (F failed), L left for the
next process-all".

The code buffers of every cache one process generates stay in its JIT
pool. NFSU2's 48 modules in one process ran out of a 4 GB pool at
SPEED2.EXE ("EXEC ALLOC FAILED", after opengl32 and shell32), and a
bigger pool leaves Wine no room to map. tests/fex-wow64/process-all-batches.sh
runs process-all in fresh processes until nothing is left.

